<h1>About ArtCard.LA</h1>
<p class="updated">ArtCard.LA is a finished public archive of 1,372 postcards by 746 artists mailed across Los Angeles from 2009–2015. Submissions are closed. It is not an ongoing project: there are no new calls, no memberships, and no programming. The site is maintained as a non-commercial record of Los Angeles mail art. If you’d like to help keep the archive online, you can <a href="{{ '/support/' | url }}">support it here</a>.</p>
<p>LA-Artist.com was started in October 2009 by two educators in LAUSD who saw firsthand how the arts could empower, uplift, and educate, yet noticed how inaccessible art remained for most people across the city and county. ArtCards were blank pre-addressed postcards distributed across Los Angeles County (in coffee shops, art galleries, community centers, schools, etc.) by volunteers and mailed via the United States Postal Service. People were encouraged to use each ArtCard as a free 4” × 6” canvas on which to create and mail in their own works of art to be published — first at LA-Artist.com, and now here at ArtCard.LA. The resulting collection of sketches, collages, photographs, and words helps to document L.A. County’s varied creative framework and influence.</p>
<p>Completed ArtCards were archived and mapped online by artist name, L.A. neighborhood and ZIP code. Over the years the collection has been exhibited and workshops have been held at Mercado La Paloma, Avenue 50 Studio, the Museum of Contemporary Art, and the Craft Contemporary on Wilshire, and it has also been printed in ArtCard Volume 1 and Volume 2, made into pins and stickers, and shared online.</p>
